_refine_special_details          
;
The crystal was determined to be four-component merohedral twin with a twin law of 0 1 0 1 0 0 0 0 -1 and inversion. Refinement of the structure with the twin law and inversion gave BASF values of 0.26(3), 0.25(3), and 0.23(3).
Thermal parameters were refined anisotropically for all non-hydrogen atoms.  
Hydrogen atoms were placed in ideal positions and refined using a riding model.
Co(p-F2-bdp) is a flexible metal-organic framework, which maintains an open structure containing solvent molecules in the as-synthesized form.
Both rapid and slow cooling of crystals of the framework to 100 K resulted in deterioration in crystal quality, which was observed in the diffraction pattern.
Cooling the crystal likeley induces non-uniform structural changes that arise from the framework flexibility. Thus, data collection was conducted at 298 K, where the crystal quality is maintained.
As a consequence of the relatively high temperature of data collection, the DEF molecules in the framework pores were severely disordered and could not be modeled.  
Consequently, the unassigned electron density was accounted for using SQUEEZE as implemented in the PLATON interface. Refinement before SQUEEZE was applied gave an R1 of 7.47% (wR2 = 25.05%), while applying SQUEEZE resulted in an R1 of 3.54% (wR2 = 9.29%).
Slight rotational disorder of the p-difluorobenzene units in the structure required the use displacement parameter restraints (RIGU and SIMU).
;
_vrf_PLAT250_mkt012s_sq          
;
PROBLEM: Large U3/U1 Ratio for Average U(i,j) Tensor ....        4.7 Note  
RESPONSE: The large average U3/U1 ratio likely arises from slight disorder from rotation and flexing of the organic linkers, which causes the displacement parameters on the linker atoms to be large in one particular direction.
;
