_refine_special_details          
;
The crystal was determined to be a inversion twin based on the value of the flack parameter. Refinement as an inversion twin gave a BASF value of 0.58(3).
Thermal parameters were refined anisotropically for all non-hydrogen atoms.  
Hydrogen atoms were placed in ideal positions and refined using a riding model.
Co(o-F2-bdp) is a flexible metal-organic framework, which maintains an open structure containing solvent molecules in the as-synthesized form.
Both rapid and slow cooling of crystals of the framework to 100 K resulted in deterioration in crystal quality, which was observed in the diffraction pattern.
Cooling the crystal likeley induces non-uniform structural changes that arise from the framework flexibility. Thus, data collection was conducted at 298 K, where the crystal quality is maintained.
As a consequence of the relatively high temperature of data collection, the DEF molecules in the framework pores were severely disordered and could not be modeled.  
Consequently, the unassigned electron density was accounted for using SQUEEZE as implemented in the PLATON interface. Refinement before SQUEEZE was applied gave an R1 of 8.82% (wR2 = 27.47%), while applying SQUEEZE resulted in an R1 of 4.92% (wR2 = 14.09%).
The o-difluorobenzene rings on the organic linkers were found to be rotationally disordered over two positions. Refinement of the relative occupancies for the two orienations for one of the two crystallographically distinct rings resulted in a site occupancy ratio of 75.6:24.4.
The site occupancies for the fluorine atoms and hydrogen atoms on the other ring were fixed 50%, as the disorder resulted from a mirror plane in the structure. The rotational disorder of the o-difluorobenzene units in the structure required the use displacement parameter (RIGU and SIMU) and distance restraints (SADI).
;
_vrf_PLAT213_mkt009s_sq          
;
PROBLEM: Atom F4              has ADP max/min Ratio .....        4.1 prolat
RESPONSE: The rotational disorder fluorine and carbon atoms on the o-difluorobenzene rings, which is aggravated by the relatively high data collection temperature, results in large values in the displacement parameters in the direction of the disorder. This causes the atomic displacement parameters
to be much larger along the direction of the disorder in comparison to those perpendicular to it.
;
